From: Code and Solder <codeandsolder@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: qca808x: experiment skipping boot-only FIFO reset

An intermittent IPQ5332/QCA8081 startup failure occurs if the first
PHY poll reports link down and the next poll (~1.06s later) link up.
That path toggles the SerDes FIFO reset and blocks all MAC RX despite
PCS/MAC link and clocks reporting normal. Healthy boots see link up
on the first poll and do not toggle the FIFO register.

As a controlled A/B experiment, skip the initial link-down FIFO reset
only until a real link-up has occurred, preserving the normal upstream
reset/deassert behavior for subsequent link transitions. This changes
one causal variable; it is not yet a confirmed hardware fix.

Diagnostic build only; do not merge upstream without testing.

Signed-off-by: Code and Solder <codeandsolder@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/phy/qcom/qca808x.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/qcom/qca808x.c
@@ -174,6 +174,8 @@
 struct qca808x_priv {
 	int led_polarity_mode;
 	int channel_id;
+	/* Avoid toggling the interface FIFO before the first actual link. */
+	bool seen_link_up;
 	/* Diagnostic-only, bounded one-time boot telemetry. */
 	u8 initial_status_samples;
 	u8 initial_link_events;
@@ -648,6 +650,25 @@
 {
 	struct qca808x_priv *priv = phydev->priv;
 	int ret;
+
+	/* The first PHY poll can race copper link negotiation. A transient
+	 * initial link-down indication is not a transition from a previously
+	 * established link; don't reset the SerDes FIFO just for this poll.
+	 * Keep the normal upstream reset on subsequent real link loss.
+	 */
+	if (phydev_id_compare(phydev, QCA8081_PHY_ID) &&
+	    !phydev->link && !priv->seen_link_up) {
+		if (priv->initial_link_events < 3) {
+			priv->initial_link_events++;
+			phydev_info(phydev,
+				    "wave7-diag: fifo[%u] initial-down-skipped state=%d\n",
+				    priv->initial_link_events, phydev->state);
+		}
+		return;
+	}
+
+	if (phydev->link)
+		priv->seen_link_up = true;
 
 	/* Assert interface sgmii fifo on link down, deassert it on link up,
 	 * the interface device address is always phy address added by 1.
